# All spoken lines of "The Pack — Part 1: The Avalanche", per scene.
# (scene, speaker, text). N = narrator, R = Range.
SCENES = [
    (1, 0, 30), (2, 30, 48), (3, 48, 98), (4, 98, 140), (5, 140, 200),
    (6, 200, 225), (7, 225, 285), (8, 285, 305), (9, 305, 360), (10, 360, 410),
    (11, 410, 460), (12, 460, 500), (13, 500, 545), (14, 545, 600), (15, 600, 635),
    (16, 635, 675), (17, 675, 735), (18, 735, 795), (19, 795, 825), (20, 825, 855),
    (21, 855, 925), (22, 925, 950),
]

LINES = [
    (1, "N", "Under an avalanche, there is no sound. No wind. Just your heartbeat, and the air in front of your face. That air is all you have. Most people who survive this are found within fifteen minutes. By the end of this video, you'll know exactly what to do if a mountain falls on you."),
    (2, "N", "Six hours earlier."),
    (3, "N", "This is Range. He's crossing a mountain ridge in the middle of winter. Seven days, alone, through some of the emptiest country on the map. He's done trips like this before. He's careful. He's prepared. And today, that won't be enough."),
    (3, "R", "Day four. Over the shoulder, down to the treeline by dark."),
    (4, "R", "Food for three days. Fuel for four. Radio, charged. Map."),
    (4, "N", "Range has a habit. Every morning, he counts what he has. Out here, knowing exactly what you've got is the difference between a plan and a guess. Remember this list. By tonight, most of it will be gone."),
    (5, "R", "No. No, no."),
    (5, "N", "Stop. That sound has a name: a whumpf. It means a weak layer deep inside the snow just collapsed under his weight. And that crack means the snow on this slope is tense, like a stretched rubber band. These are the mountain's loudest warnings. Most avalanches that bury people are set off by the people they bury. If you hear a whumpf, or see cracks shooting out from your feet, don't keep going. Back off the slope the way you came. Gently. And find safer ground."),
    (6, "R", "Ah!"),
    (6, "N", "A slab avalanche can reach the speed of a car on a highway in just a few seconds. You can't outrun it."),
    (7, "N", "Let's stop time for a second. If you're ever caught in an avalanche, here's what to do. First, fight. Try to get to the side of the slide, where the snow moves slower. If you can, grab a tree. Kick and swim hard to stay near the top. Then comes the most important part. When you feel the snow starting to slow down, push one arm straight up toward the light. And put your other hand in front of your mouth and nose. Why? Because the moment the snow stops, you won't be able to move. That hand in front of your face makes a small air pocket. That pocket is what you'll breathe."),
    (8, "N", "And then... nothing. Just a quiet mountain, as if nothing happened."),
    (9, "R", "Can't... move."),
    (9, "N", "Here's what surprises most people. Avalanche snow isn't soft and fluffy like fresh powder. While it's moving, the snow grinds against itself. The moment it stops, it packs together and sets almost like concrete. Even a shallow layer can pin you completely. And the biggest danger isn't the cold. It's running out of air. That's why those fifteen minutes matter so much."),
    (9, "N", "So the first job is simple. Slow your breathing down. Panic burns air."),
    (10, "N", "When you tumble in an avalanche, you can lose all sense of direction. You might be lying on your side and not even know it. Dig the wrong way, and you waste the air you have. There's an old trick for this. Let a little spit dribble out of your mouth. Gravity always wins. Whichever way it runs is down. Dig the other way. It won't dig you out. But it can stop you digging the wrong way."),
    (10, "R", "Sideways. I'm on my side."),
    (11, "N", "Range got lucky. His raised hand ended up close to the surface, so he could dig. Many people who are fully buried can't free themselves at all. They survive only because someone digs them out. That's why people who travel in avalanche country never go alone. They carry an avalanche beacon, a probe and a shovel. And a partner who knows how to use them. Range was alone. That was his first mistake. He doesn't plan to make a second."),
    (12, "R", "Head... fine. Arms... ribs... Bruised. Not broken. Legs work."),
    (12, "N", "Before he does anything else, Range checks his body from head to toe. After a fall, adrenaline can hide an injury. A broken bone you don't notice now can become a disaster in an hour."),
    (13, "R", "No pack. No map. No radio. No food. Knife. Fire steel. Emergency blanket. Notebook. Two energy bars. Water... half a bottle."),
    (13, "N", "This morning, he had a plan. Now he has what fits in his pockets. That's why experienced travelers keep a few essentials on their body, not only in the pack. A pack can be lost. Pockets stay with you."),
    (14, "R", "Way back's buried. Down, then."),
    (14, "N", "Survival experts use a simple rule called the rule of threes. You can live about three minutes without air. About three hours without shelter in harsh cold. About three days without water. And about three weeks without food. Look at that list again. Range just got his air back. The next thing that can kill him isn't hunger. It's the cold, and the night that's coming. His damp clothes and the rising wind will pull heat from his body fast. So before food, before finding the trail, before anything else, he needs shelter."),
    (15, "N", "He stays at the edge of the slide, close to rocks and trees. Then, with less than an hour of light left, he sees something nature doesn't make. A perfectly straight line."),
    (15, "R", "A roof."),
    (16, "N", "An old hunter's cabin. No smoke from the chimney. No tracks around the door. Nobody has been here for years."),
    (16, "R", "Somebody hunted something up here."),
    (17, "N", "When you're cold and tired, an old shelter looks like a gift. And it might be. But old buildings can also kill you. So before you go in, check three things. First, the roof. Snow is heavy. Wet snow piled on a roof can weigh as much as a car. Look for beams that sag or crack. If one part of the roof is bending, don't sleep under it."),
    (17, "R", "I sleep on this side. Away from you."),
    (18, "N", "Second, the floor. Old boards rot. Test each step before you trust it. And look for signs of animals: droppings, chewed wood, a nest. You don't want to share a small room with something that got there first."),
    (18, "N", "Third, and this one matters most: the chimney. If it's blocked, smoke can't get out. And smoke carries a gas called carbon monoxide. You can't see it. You can't smell it. It gives you a headache, makes you sleepy, and it can kill you in your sleep. Before you light any fire indoors, make sure the smoke has a clear way out."),
    (18, "R", "Now we've got a chimney."),
    (19, "N", "A small fire. Nothing big. Just enough to dry his gloves and push the cold back a few feet. Tomorrow, keeping this fire alive will become a fight of its own."),
    (20, "R", "Day four. Lost the trail. Lost the map. Lost the radio. Still have my head. Still have a roof."),
    (20, "N", "Out here, the most important survival tool isn't in the pack. It's staying calm enough to think."),
    (21, "N", "Let's go over what Range learned today. One: a whumpf, or cracks shooting out from your feet, mean the slope is unstable. Get off it, gently, the way you came. Two: if you're caught, fight to the side and stay near the top. As the snow slows, push one hand up and put the other in front of your face for an air pocket. Three: if you don't know which way is up, let a little spit fall. Gravity always tells the truth. Four: shelter first. Cold kills faster than hunger. Five: before you trust an old shelter, check the roof, the floor and the chimney. And one bonus rule: never travel in avalanche country alone."),
    (22, "R", "Guess I'm not alone up here."),
    (22, "N", "Next time on The Pack: the cold starts counting."),
]

if __name__ == "__main__":
    n = sum(len(t) for _, s, t in LINES if s == "N")
    r = sum(len(t) for _, s, t in LINES if s == "R")
    print(len(LINES), "lines; narrator", n, "range", r, "total", n + r)
